      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">Transparent Cost Matrix &amp; Tariff Breakdown for Packers and Movers from Bangalore to Thalassery</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;"><p>The fare structure for freight transport is layered to reflect the true cost of moving goods. At the foundation lies the <strong>base distance charge</strong>, a fixed fee that covers the mileage between origin and destination. This is usually calculated per kilometer or mile and varies with the route’s length and traffic conditions.</p>

<p style="font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 12px;">Next, <strong>weight and volume slabs</strong> come into play.</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 16px 0 20px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;"><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>Carriers set thresholds (e.g., 0–50 kg, 51–200 kg, 201–500 kg) and assign a per‑kilogram rate that decreases as the load grows larger.</span></li><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>Volume is measured in cubic meters; if the cargo is bulky but light, a volume‑based rate may supersede the weight rate.</span></li><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>This dual‑slab system ensures that both density and mass are accounted for.</span></li></ul>

<p>On top of these base costs, a <strong>fuel surcharge</strong> is applied to buffer fluctuations in diesel or gasoline prices. The surcharge is typically a percentage of the base distance charge and is recalculated monthly. It keeps the freight company’s operating costs stable without passing the entire burden to the customer.</p>

<p style="font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 12px;">Finally, <strong>extra‑service fees</strong> cover specialized handling.</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 16px 0 20px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;"><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>Packing fees apply when the carrier provides protective materials or custom packaging.</span></li><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>Loading and unloading charges are assessed when the customer’s truck is not available, or the freight requires palletizing, crane use, or other equipment.</span></li><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>Each of these fees is itemized so customers can see exactly what they are paying for.</span></li></ul>

<h3>Sample Tariff Table</h3>
<p>A typical tariff table might list the following columns: <strong>Distance (km)</strong>, <strong>Weight slab (kg)</strong>, <strong>Volume slab (m³)</strong>, <strong>Base rate (USD)</strong>, <strong>Fuel surcharge (%)</strong>, <strong>Packing fee (USD)</strong>, <strong>Loading fee (USD)</strong>, <strong>Unloading fee (USD)</strong>. Each row represents a specific combination of distance and load, allowing shippers to quickly identify the applicable rate.</p>

